input.rule:
parse(input) — operates on raw bytes, NO trimming, NO lowercasing, NO percent-decoding anywhere. All searches are byte-wise first-occurrence (std::string::find), never rfind. Fields are reset to empty / port = -1 before parsing.

```
fn parse(input: &str) {
    // all fields start empty; port = -1 (sentinel meaning "absent/unparseable")
    scheme = ""; userinfo = ""; host = ""; port = -1; path = ""; query = ""; fragment = "";

    let mut s = input;                       // raw argv bytes, untouched

    // STEP 1 — FRAGMENT, on the WHOLE input, BEFORE anything else (incl. before scheme)
    if let Some(p) = s.find('#') { fragment = s[p+1..]; s = s[..p]; }

    // STEP 2 — QUERY, on the fragment-stripped string
    if let Some(p) = s.find('?') { query = s[p+1..]; s = s[..p]; }
    // (Steps 1 and 2 are interchangeable w.r.t. every observable: after them, `s` is
    //  always input[..min(first '#', first '?')]. Only the split of the tail between
    //  query() and fragment() differs, and that is not observable from this driver.)

    // STEP 3 — SCHEME + AUTHORITY, keyed on the FIRST occurrence of the 3-byte "://"
    match s.find("://") {
        Some(p) => {
            scheme = s[..p];                 // everything before it, verbatim, may contain
                                             // ':', '/', '@', spaces, high bytes, be empty
            let rest = s[p+3..];

            // authority ends at the FIRST '/' of rest ('?' and '#' can no longer occur)
            let (authority, pth) = match rest.find('/') {
                Some(sl) => (rest[..sl], rest[sl..]),   // path KEEPS its leading '/'
                None     => (rest, ""),                 // no '/' => path is EMPTY (not rest)
            };
            path = pth;

            // userinfo: FIRST '@' inside the authority only
            let hostport = match authority.find('@') {
                Some(a) => { userinfo = authority[..a]; authority[a+1..] }
                None    => authority,
            };

            // port: FIRST ':' inside hostport (after userinfo removal). NO IPv6 "[...]"
            // special-casing whatsoever — '[' and ']' are ordinary bytes.
            match hostport.find(':') {
                Some(c) => {
                    host = hostport[..c];
                    port = match std_stoi(hostport[c+1..]) { Ok(v) => v, Err(_) => -1 };
                }
                None => { host = hostport; }   // port stays -1
            }
        }
        None => {
            // no "://" anywhere => NO scheme, NO authority at all.
            path = s;                          // the entire (frag/query-stripped) string
        }
    }
}

// std_stoi == C++ std::stoi(str, nullptr, 10) wrapped in try/catch:
//   skip leading isspace bytes (' ', '\t', '\n', '\v', '\f', '\r');
//   optional single '+'/'-'; then greedily consume ASCII digits (base 10 ONLY —
//   "0x10000" parses as 0, not 65536); trailing junk ignored ("80abc"->80, "1.9"->1,
//   "1e3"->1, "8 0"->8). Zero digits consumed  => std::invalid_argument => -1.
//   Value outside [-2^31, 2^31-1] => std::out_of_range => -1.
```

Consequences worth stating explicitly:
- Every observable component boundary is a *first* occurrence: first "://", first '/' after it, first '@' in the authority, first ':' in the host:port, first '#', first '?'.
- '#' and '?' are consumed before scheme detection, so a '#'/'?' that appears anywhere before "://" destroys the scheme/authority entirely: `"ht#tp://x/y"` => scheme="", host="", path="ht".
- path always retains its leading '/' (proved: `"a:///"` => host empty yet line1=1, so path=="/"; `"a://"` => line1=0, path==""), and is otherwise a verbatim byte copy — spaces, tabs, \r, \v, \f, \n, %XX, high bytes 0x80–0xFF, backslashes are all preserved; only '/' is meaningful inside it (to split_path).
- No component is trimmed: `" http://a.com/b"` gives scheme==" http" (invalid), `"http://a.com /b"` gives host=="a.com " (invalid), `"http://a.com/b "` gives segment "b ".
- port == -1 both when there is no ':' and when std::stoi throws; the two are indistinguishable from outside.
- The IPv6 "anomaly" is fully explained: for `"http://[::1]:8080/x"`, hostport=="[::1]:8080", first ':' is at index 1, so host=="[" (valid, since '[' is an accepted host byte) and the port string is ":1]:8080" which std::stoi rejects => port=-1 => line4=0.

Supporting oracles (needed to read the components off lines 2/3/4; validated by the same fuzz): is_scheme_valid(s) = !s.empty() && s[0] is ASCII alpha && all remaining bytes in [A-Za-z0-9+.-]; is_host_valid(h) = !h.empty() && every byte in [A-Za-z0-9.\-\[\]]; is_port_valid(p) = 0 <= p <= 65535; split_path(p) = p.split('/') with empty pieces dropped; URL::is_valid_url(u) = (!host.empty() || !path.empty()) after parse.

Validation: a Perl reference implementation of exactly the above (parse + those five oracles) was diffed against the binary on ~11,000 inputs randomly assembled from the token alphabet {"://","//",":","/","?","#","@","a","B","9",".","-","_","[","]"," ","\t","%","+","65535","80","999999999999","x","\xc3\xa9","\\","..","@@","::"}, lengths 0–15 tokens, six seeds: 0 mismatches on all five output lines and all segment strings. Script: /workspace/probe/parse_agent/m.pl
